.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt HALT 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm halt ,
.Nm poweroff ,
.Nm reboot
.Nd stop, power off or restart the machine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm halt
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl n
.Nm poweroff
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl n
.Nm reboot
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl n
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These are one program, told apart by the name it is run under, exactly as
System V's were.
.Pp
Outside runlevels 0 and 6 they are a polite way of spelling
.Xr shutdown 8 :
the command becomes
.Ic shutdown Fl r Ns | Ns Fl P Ns | Ns Fl H Cm now ,
so the request goes to
.Xr init 8 ,
which stops the services and takes the machine down in order.
Inside runlevel 0 or 6 the shutdown is already happening - these are what the
last rc script would call - so the kernel call is made directly.
.Fl f
asks for that directly from anywhere, and is what to use when
.Xr init 8
is not answering.
.Pp
The three names differ in what the machine does once it has stopped:
.Bl -tag -width poweroff -offset indent
.It Nm halt
stop the machine, leaving the power on.
.It Nm poweroff
stop it and cut the power.
.It Nm reboot
start it again.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-p"
.It Fl f
Make the kernel call now, without asking
.Xr init 8
to stop anything first.
Nothing is given a chance to save what it is holding.
.It Fl p
Power off rather than halt.
.Nm halt
only;
.Nm poweroff
is this option with a name.
.It Fl n
Do not
.Xr sync 2
before the kernel call.
.El
.Pp
.Fl w , Fl d
and
.Fl i
are accepted and ignored: nothing here writes wtmp, and there are no modules to
unload or interfaces to take down first.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A program that has done its work does not return at all.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr init 8 ,
.Xr shutdown 8 ,
.Xr telinit 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Only root can do any of this.
.Pp
.Fl f
is a lost-work switch, not a faster one: filesystems are not unmounted, and a
daemon holding data in memory is killed with it.
Without
.Fl f
the same command is orderly, and is what to type.
